+\subsection{Converting the Human Model and Comparing Muscle Force}
+
+MyoConverter is an open-source program that translates OpenSim joints and muscle paths into MuJoCo format \citep{caggiano_myosuite_2022,wang_myosim_2022}. I used it to convert the Gait2392 human model. The converted model reversed two hip actions and assigned eight muscles a maximum active force of only \qty{1}{\newton}. I reversed the affected hinge axes and restored the force scales from OpenSim. At the knee, a misplaced path point prevented the rectus femoris from following the patella as the joint extended. I replaced it with a patella-tracking point from the vastus medialis. These repairs matter because a change in the path can change the torque produced by a given muscle force. Foot contact was set separately. I retained the six foot--ground contact pairs for loaded trials and removed all ground pairs for suspension. Appendix~\ref{sec:app_conversion_repairs} identifies the repaired muscles, the disabled hip rotators, and the joint constraints.
+
+How closely did the converted muscles reproduce the forces estimated in OpenSim? I took 121 poses from a reference walk and applied the OpenSim joint angles and estimated muscle activations to the MuJoCo model, one pose at a time \citep{opensim_static_optimization_docs}. At each pose, MuJoCo used the routed length and prescribed activation to calculate active and passive tension with its rigid-tendon muscle model. Eight of twelve right-side muscles followed the OpenSim force pattern with mean forces within \qty{10}{\percent}. For rectus femoris, similar means (\qty{48}{\newton} in OpenSim and \qty{52}{\newton} in MuJoCo) concealed a large error at one pose (\qty{96}{\newton} versus \qty{416}{\newton}). For the short head of biceps femoris, the forces rose and fell together, but the MuJoCo mean was about \qty{85}{\percent} higher. For these two muscles a constant equal to the OpenSim mean gave a smaller squared error than the MuJoCo estimates. The reference used a subject-scaled OpenSim model, whereas the conversion began with the generic model. Muscle dimensions, routing, and the force calculations therefore all affect this comparison. No neural controller supplied the activations. Appendix~\ref{sec:muscle-models} describes the force equations and Appendix~\ref{sec:muscle-models-compare} gives the results for each muscle.
 
