+The MuJoCo version for Gait2392 reproduced the output of the adapted Python neural equations to within $3.34\times10^{-16}$ over 2,000 prescribed input steps. The coupled model did not reproduce the published gait. An early 960-trial search that rewarded trial duration and speed selected a tumbling trajectory. A later comparison corrected the order of the muscle length and velocity inputs, read normal foot load, and reset phase at heel-contact onset. With the body, initial state, and \qty{70}{\percent} support held fixed, both the original and refitted pattern-formation weights produced a fall within half a second, defined by center-of-mass height dropping below \qty{80}{\percent} of its initial value. The transfer changed the body, muscle groups and their feedback, balance control, and search objective. These comparisons establish agreement between neural implementations under prescribed inputs and identify specific sensory-interface errors. Determining why the adapted body fell requires separating those changes.
+
+I also tested the effect of transferring settings between two dynamics engines in a separate SCONE gait example. Hyfydy is a musculoskeletal dynamics engine available as a separately licensed SCONE plug-in.\footnote{Hyfydy documents activation and the trial license at \url{https://hyfydy.com/downloads/}.} I used a trial license for these comparisons. With the same reflex controller and the same four-second test duration, settings tuned for Hyfydy completed the trial in Hyfydy and reached \qty{1.96}{\second} in OpenSim. Settings tuned for OpenSim reached \qty{3.23}{\second} in OpenSim and \qty{1.23}{\second} in Hyfydy. The engines differ in muscle dynamics, foot contact, and numerical integration. Controller settings that work with one implementation therefore require testing on the other.
 
